[THOUGHT]  
The dialogue describes a classic presentation of benign paroxysmal positional vertigo (BPPV): episodic, brief, spinning vertigo triggered by specific head movements (e.g., rolling over in bed, bending down, looking up), with no associated auditory symptoms (no hearing loss, tinnitus, or ear fullness). The Dix-Hallpike maneuver was performed and yielded a positive result — right-sided horizontal nystagmus upon moving from sitting to supine position — which is consistent with right anterior or horizontal canal BPPV (though horizontal nystagmus in Dix-Hallpike is more commonly associated with horizontal canal BPPV, especially if geotropic and persistent; however, the report specifies *right-sided horizontal nystagmus* in the context of Dix-Hallpike, which warrants careful interpretation — but per clinical documentation, it is recorded as positive and suggestive of BPPV). No spontaneous nystagmus was assessed or reported. There is no evidence of central nervous system involvement (e.g., no headache, diplopia, dysarthria, ataxia, or long-duration vertigo), and no red-flag features.

[SUMMARY]  
**Clinical Impression:**  
- **Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo (BPPV), likely right horizontal semicircular canal variant**, based on:  
  • Episodic, positional, spinning vertigo triggered by head movement (rolling over in bed, bending, looking up);  
  • Positive Dix-Hallpike maneuver demonstrating right-sided horizontal nystagmus;  
  • Absence of auditory symptoms (no hearing loss, tinnitus, or aural fullness);  
  • No reported neurological deficits or red-flag features.  

**Next Steps (implied/standard of care):**  
- Canalith repositioning maneuver (e.g., Barbecue (Lempert) maneuver for horizontal canal BPPV, or Gufoni maneuver if apogeotropic variant suspected);  
- Avoidance of provoking head positions pending treatment;  
- Reassessment post-maneuver for resolution of nystagmus and symptoms;  
- Consider vestibular rehabilitation if refractory;  
- Counsel patient regarding natural history (self-limited but recurrent) and low risk of serious underlying pathology given negative screening.  

No further urgent neurotologic or imaging workup indicated at this time.

